msgid "gcode_options_button_text"
msgstr "G-code Options"

msgid "gcode_options_button_tooltip"
msgstr "<b>@@</b> allows selection of options for the G-code window to the left. (Same as right-clicking in the G-code window)<span color='#0012da'>\n    <b>Set Start Line</b>\n    <b>Show/Hide Line</b>\n    <b>Show/Hide G-code color high-lighting</b></span>"

msgid "conv_edit_job_duplicate_button_tooltip"
msgstr "<b>@@:</b> creates a duplicate of the selected <i>step</i> in the conversational list and places it one row below. If a <i>file</i> row is selected, the file and all its steps will be duplicated below.\n <span color='#16861f'>Note: The <i><b>duplicated icon</b></i> will appear next to the title of the duplicated step. Duplicating is handy when the same step is needed but with minor changes.</span> <img>1</img>"

msgid "msg_cycle_start_text"
msgstr "Cycle Start"

msgid "msg_cycle_start_tooltip"
msgstr "The Cycle Start button is used to start a program. While running a program, the LED in the upper right-hand corner of the button is illuminated. <span color='#16861f'><i>Note: The LED will flash during Feedhold and optional pauses.</i></span>"

msgid "msg_reset_text"
msgstr "Reset"

msgid "msg_reset_tooltip"
msgstr "Reset brings the machine <font color='#16861f'><b>out of an E-stop condition</b></font>, <font color='#16861f'><b>resets the robot program</b></font> and <font color='#16861f'><b>rewinds the TRPL program</b></font>.\n\nThe Reset text flashes when the machine is first powered on, or after an emergency stop. When flashing and after the machine has been powered on again, clicking <font color='#16861f'><b>Reset starts and verifies communication between the machine and the controller</b></font>."

msgid "msg_single_block_text"
msgstr "Execute Single Block"

msgid "msg_single_block_tooltip"
msgstr "<b>@@:</b> When active (LED - on), modifies the behavior of Cycle Start to executes <b><i>one line (or command)</i></b> of the robot program. Program execution is then paused, the Cycle Start LED will flash, prompting the user to execute the next (single) line. This feature may be turned on or off before running a program or during program execution. <span color='#16861f'><i>NOTE: Non-motion lines are ignored by single block mode: the interface will skip comment lines and non-motion commands.</i></span>"

msgid "msg_optional_pause_text"
msgstr "Optional Pause Execution"

msgid "msg_optional_pause_tooltip"
msgstr "<b>@@:</b> When active (LED - on), program execution will stop at an Optional Pause in the robot program. The machine stops at the Optional Pause line and the Cycle Start LED flashes. Cycle Start will resume program execution."

msgid "msg_feedhold_text"
msgstr "Feedhold"

msgid "msg_feedhold_tooltip"
msgstr "When active (LED - on), pauses machine motion, the Cycle Start LED will begin flashing. To turn Feedhold off, click Cycle Start. The Feedhold button works during program execution or during manual data input (MDI) moves. Feedhold has no effect when the machine is not moving."

msgid "msg_stop_text"
msgstr "Instant Stop"

msgid "msg_stop_tooltip"
msgstr "Stops all machine motion. If clicked while running a program or during an MDI move, the machine stops. Stop doesn’t change the current state of the machine's digital outputs."

msgid "add_new_waypoint_from_current_position_text"
msgstr "Create a new waypoint from the robot's current position"

msgid "add_new_waypoint_from_current_position_tooltip"
msgstr "Creates a new waypoint corresponding to the robot's current position.  The waypoint may be defined as either <b>global</b>, (accessible to all robot programs) or <b>program</b>, (accessible only to the current robot program).  Furthermore, the waypoint can be recorded as a pose type (affected by coordinate system offsets) or a joint type (unaffected by offsets)."

msgid "conv_move_type_radio_text"
msgstr "Move Type"

msgid "conv_move_type_radio_tooltip"
msgstr "Linear moves cause the robot to move along a straight line between waypoints.  Joint moves allow the robot to move more quickly but along an arbitrary path - the end effector will not travel a straight line.  Unless controlled path planning (e.g. for welding, glue deposition, etc.) is required, joint moves are usually faster/more efficient."

msgid "conv_move_add_to_program_text"
msgstr "Add Move to Selected Waypoint"

msgid "conv_move_add_to_program_tooltip"
msgstr "The Add Move to Selected Waypoint to Program button adds a move command to the currently loaded program.  The move command will cause the robot to move to the waypoint that was selected when the button was pressed.  The move will be either a Linear or Joint move (depending on the radio button selected on the Move tab of the conversational notebook) and will use the velocity and acceleration settings on that screen."

msgid "conv_move_pose_waypoints_radio_text"
msgstr "Pose Waypoints"

msgid "conv_move_pose_waypoints_radio_tooltip"
msgstr "Pose waypoints use the Cartesian position of the robot's end effector to define the waypoint.  The Cartesian position is defined by x, y, z position as well as the three Euler angles (think of pitch, roll, and yaw).   Pose waypoints are affected by work offsets.  If a \"pose\"-type waypoint is defined in one work offset system, and that offset is subsequently modified, or a different offset system is made active, the robot will move to a different physical position when it next moves to that waypoint.  A typical use for a program waypoint would be the pick position near the vise of a milling machine.  If the milling machine vise is repositioned, the machinist would need to adjust the (for example) G54 work offset on the mill as well as the work offset on the robot in order for the mill to machine the part correctly and the robot to pick the workpiece up correctly.  Using pose waypoints and work offsets means that the operator doesn't need to rewrite robot programs when a reference position (such as a vise) is moved - the operator simply needs to change the work offset.  For more information on pose and joint waypoint types, please see this video: www.youtube.com/xxxxx"

msgid "conv_move_joint_waypoints_radio_text"
msgstr "Joint Waypoints"

msgid "conv_move_joint_waypoints_radio_tooltip"
msgstr "Joint waypoints use the robot's joint angles to define the waypoint.  Waypoints of this type are not affected by work offset coordinate systems.  Changing work offset systems or work offset values will not change the behavior of the robot when moving between \"joint\" waypoints.  For users familiar with CNC machines, joint waypoints are analogous to G53 (machine coordinate) positions.  For more information on joint and pose waypoint types, please see Tormach's YouTube channel."

msgid "conv_move_program_waypoints_radio_text"
msgstr "Program Waypoints"

msgid "conv_move_program_waypoints_radio_tooltip"
msgstr "Program waypoints are defined only within a program and aren't available when a different program is loaded.  When this button is selected, the waypoint list shows all waypoints that have been defined in the currently loaded program.  Loading a different program will cause the list of program waypoints to change.  The list of program waypoints will  be empty when a new program is created until the user creates program waypoints for that new program.  For more information on waypoint types, please see www.youtube.com/xxxxx"

msgid "conv_move_global_waypoints_radio_text"
msgstr "Global Waypoints"

msgid "conv_move_global_waypoints_radio_tooltip"
msgstr "Global waypoints are defined system-wide, are available to all programs.  When this button is selected, the waypoint list shows all global waypoint.  For more information on waypoint types, please see Tormach's YouTube channel."

msgid "uniform_velocity_slider_combo_text"
msgstr "Limit maximum velocity"

msgid "uniform_velocity_slider_combo_tooltip"
msgstr "The Uniform Velocity Override slider changes the velocity of all robot motion (including jogging) by a specified percentage. The Max Vel 100% button restores the velocity setting to the maximum velocity allowed by jogging or by the robot program."

msgid "feedrate_override_slider_combo_text"
msgstr "Limit programmed velocity"

msgid "feedrate_override_slider_combo_tooltip"
msgstr "The Velocity Scale Override slider changes the programmed velocity of linear moves by a specified percentage.  The Vel Scale 100% button will restore the feed rate to 100% of the commanded velocity."

msgid "jograte_slider_text"
msgstr "Limit jogging velocity"

msgid "jograte_slider_tooltip"
msgstr "Jog speed is affected by both the Jog Speed slider and is further limited by the Maximum Velocity (in the Persistent Controls section) if the Maximum Velocity slider is set to a lower speed than the Jog Speed slider."

msgid "jog_type_switch_text"
msgstr "Switch jog type"

msgid "jog_type_switch_tooltip"
msgstr "Jog behavior is affected by the Cont/Incr button, and either occurs as a continuous move for as long as the jog button is held down, or as an incremental move whose distance is equal to the currently selected Step Size button. The Cont/Incr button switches between those two modes, and that button’s LED indicates whether the robot is in continuous or incremental mode.   *Note: *Clicking a Step Size button selects incremental mode if the current mode is continuous, and dragging the Jog Velocity slider selects continuous mode if the current mode is incremental."

msgid "jog_step_size_0.100_text"
msgstr "Set step size to 0.100"

msgid "jog_step_size_0.100_tooltip"
msgstr "Use this button to set the step size for incremental jogging to 0.100.\n\n<font color='#16861f'><b>Note</b></font>: Clicking a Step Size button selects incremental mode if the current mode is continuous, and dragging the Jog Velocity slider selects continuous mode if the current mode is incremental."

msgid "jog_step_size_1.000_text"
msgstr "Set step size to 1.000"

msgid "jog_step_size_1.000_tooltip"
msgstr "Use this button to set the step size for incremental jogging to 1.000.\n\n<font color='#16861f'><b>Note</b></font>: Clicking a Step Size button selects incremental mode if the current mode is continuous, and dragging the Jog Velocity slider selects continuous mode if the current mode is incremental."


msgid "jog_step_size_10.00_text"
msgstr "Set step size to 10.00"

msgid "jog_step_size_10.00_tooltip"
msgstr "Use this button to set the step size for incremental jogging to 10.00.\n\n<font color='#16861f'><b>Note</b></font>: Clicking a Step Size button selects incremental mode if the current mode is continuous, and dragging the Jog Velocity slider selects continuous mode if the current mode is incremental."


msgid "jog_step_size_100.0_text"
msgstr "Set step size to 100.0"

msgid "jog_step_size_100.0_tooltip"
msgstr "Use this button to set the step size for incremental jogging to 100.0.\n\n<font color='#16861f'><b>Note</b></font>: Clicking a Step Size button selects incremental mode if the current mode is continuous, and dragging the Jog Velocity slider selects continuous mode if the current mode is incremental."

msgid "dro_zero_x_text"
msgstr "Zero <font color='#16861f'><b>X</b></font> DRO"

msgid "dro_zero_x_tooltip"
msgstr "Click and hold the Zero X button to set the robot's current X position to zero in the currently active user frame.  If no user frame is active, this button generates an error."

msgid "dro_zero_y_text"
msgstr "Zero <font color='#16861f'><b>Y</b></font> DRO"

msgid "dro_zero_y_tooltip"
msgstr "Click and hold the Zero Y button to set the robot's current Y position to zero in the currently active user frame.  If no user frame is active, this button generates an error."

msgid "dro_zero_z_text"
msgstr "Zero <font color='#16861f'><b>Z</b></font> DRO"

msgid "dro_zero_z_tooltip"
msgstr "Click and hold the Zero Z button to set the robot's current Z position to zero in the currently active user frame.  If no user frame is active, this button generates an error."

msgid "dro_zero_a_text"
msgstr "Zero <font color='#16861f'><b>A</b></font> DRO"

msgid "dro_zero_a_tooltip"
msgstr "Click and hold the Zero A button to set the robot's current A position to zero in the currently active user frame.  If no user frame is active, this button generates an error."

msgid "dro_zero_b_text"
msgstr "Zero <font color='#16861f'><b>B</b></font> DRO"

msgid "dro_zero_b_tooltip"
msgstr "Click and hold the Zero B button to set the robot's current B position to zero in the currently active user frame.  If no user frame is active, this button generates an error."

msgid "dro_zero_c_text"
msgstr "Zero <font color='#16861f'><b>C</b></font> DRO"

msgid "dro_zero_c_tooltip"
msgstr "Click and hold the Zero C button to set the robot's current C position to zero in the currently active user frame.  If no user frame is active, this button generates an error."

msgid "add_user_frame_text"
msgstr "Create new user frame"

msgid "add_user_frame_tooltip"
msgstr "Select Add Frame to create a new user frame. In the window that displays, you can name the new frame.\n\n<font color='#16861f'><b>Note:</b></font> Creating a new frame doesn't automatically apply that frame. You must either select the frame in the User Frame drop-down menu on the “Position Status Readout” group of the persistent controls, or use the change_user_frame() command in a robot program."

msgid "set_command_conversational_notebook_text"
msgstr "Set digital output states"

msgid "set_command_conversational_notebook_tooltip"
msgstr "The <font color='#16861f'><b>Set tab</b></font> allows you to add commands that change the state of digital outputs to the robot program. You can rename the digital outputs on the Status tab of the main notebook, which could make robot programs easier to read or understand.\n\nFor example, you could rename digital output 1 to <i>gripper_one</i>."

msgid "subprogram_conversational_notebook_text"
msgstr "Create sub programs"

msgid "subprogram_conversatisonal_notebook_tooltip"
msgstr "Sub programs allow you to organize robot programs into small, logical blocks of code that may be called one or more times during the main program. The <font color='#16861f'><b>Sub Program tab</b></font> allows you to create and call sub programs conversationally (without writing Python robot code <i>“by hand”</i>)."

msgid "add_waypoint_popup_close_singularity_text"
msgstr "Close to singularity"

msgid "add_waypoint_popup_close_singularity_tooltip"
msgstr "Current cartesian pose is close to singularity. Consider using joint goal instead."

msgid "run_subprogram_text"
msgstr "Run subprogram"

msgid "run_subprogram_tooltip"
msgstr "Click to run a subprogram from your loaded program. Subprograms will only appear if they require no parameters or all parameters have a default value. This control can help you quickly execute small, logical blocks of robot actions."


msgid "msg_frame_user_or_tool_toggle_text"
msgstr "Jogging with respect to user or tool frame coordinates"

msgid "msg_frame_user_or_tool_toggle_tooltip"
msgstr "When jogging the robot via the cartesian jog buttons on the Jog tab or by using keyboard shortcuts, this button will determine whether the cartesian jog directions align with the active user frame or tool frame.  If no user or tool frame is active, all cartesian jogging motions will be relative to the base frame of the robot and this button has no effect."

msgid "msg_jog_ignore_probe_text"
msgstr "Ignore probe input while jogging"

msgid "msg_jog_ignore_probe_tooltip"
msgstr "Digital input 1 is reserved for a touch probe.  By default the robot will not move when the touch probe signal is present in order to prevent users from damaging the probe.  If the robot is jogged and the probe input changes state, the robot will stop its motion.  To jog away from the object that triggered the probe this button can be used to allow motion with the probe in a triggered state.  Touch probes can be active high or active low, make sure to set the setting on the settings tab appropriately before using a touch probe."

msgid "msg_exit_text"
msgstr "Exit"

msgid "msg_exit_tooltip"
msgstr "The exit button will close the PathPilot user interface.  Make sure to E-stop or turn off the robot's power supply before exiting the user interface."

